{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>For he interpreted well.</b> Onkelos renders (Onkelos on Genesis 40:16) כִּי יָפֶה פָּתַר — for he interpreted beautifully. Similarly, \"Teach me good taste and knowledge\" (Psalms 119:66), and similarly, \"For they are beautiful\" (Genesis 6:2) — יָפוֹת. The reason for saying so is that this man was contemptuous of Joseph, thinking that he could never interpret it, and that he would not tell him the dream unless he saw that he had interpreted his fellow's dream beautifully and properly. Or one may say: the chief baker saw that he had interpreted for good, and he rejoiced and told his dream, for he had been more distressed over it than his fellow." "<b>Salei ḥori.</b> Baskets of peeled canes woven hole by hole — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 40:16). And the Gaon Rav Saadia explained: baskets of white bread like the king's bread, from the expression \"and not now shall his face grow pale\" (Isaiah 29:22), from the Aramaic, where the word for white is חִוָּר. And this is the correct interpretation, for there was in all the baskets bread of the king, and in the uppermost basket all kinds of Pharaoh's food that was the work of a baker. And the language of our Sages in the Mishnah (Beitzah 2:6) is: coarse loaves and white breads (פִּתִּין גְּרִיצִים וְחִוָּרֵי). And they said in the Jerusalem Talmud (ibid.): the Rabbis learn it from this verse: \"and behold, three baskets of white bread (סַלֵּי חֹרִי) upon my head\"."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {0 "The phrase כִּי טוֹב פָּתַר is from Genesis 40:16.", 1 "Ramban quotes the Mishnah in Beitzah 2:6 using the term חִוָּרֵי, linking it to his reading of חֹרִי as 'white.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:32.333302705Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}